Z||000000||Standard||02-21-2001||02:39 AM||||Just went out and got the fiberglass stuff, expanding foam and construction paper.  Instead of getting the repair kit I got the seprate resin and 8 sq. feet of cloth.  Probably overkill, but it was about the same price as the repair kit and from the wording on the box it sounded like the repair kit came with fiberglass mat.. Total came to a little less than 20 bucks for everything.  Of course, as soon as I get home I got into the workshop to look for some duct tape and I see two huge cans of expanding foam and a bondo fiberglass repair kit...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> and no duct tape, of course.  Ah well... <P>Gonna start makin' the foam plug after I wake up.  <P>------------------<BR>Everybody wants a normal life and a cool car.. <B>Most people settle for the car</B><P>I'm not dumb.  I just have a command of thoroughly useless information<BR>        <BR>AOL: LastMinuteBastrd<BR>AIM: SavedByJebus    -- you'll understand if you watch The Simpsons :D||||reg||1
Z||000001||stimpy||02-21-2001||09:59 AM||||Go get em! I'll be keeping tabs. What kind of speaker did you get?||||reg||1
Z||000002||sqoach||02-21-2001||11:41 AM||||me too! me too!<P>i haven't started yet, but i'm planning on it this week. not sure if i want to go the fiberglass way or not. i've never worked with the stuff. might try something different.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig2.JPG"> <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||10
Z||000003||Standard||02-21-2001||12:44 PM||||I'm gonna be using a Pioneer 10" sub.  Got it from a guy I used to work with for 50 bucks last summer, box might be a little too small for it, but oh well.  Just picked up a Pioneer GM-X542 amp at Best Buy 20 minutes ago, too.  150w bridged at 4 ohms.  Open item, 85 bucks.  My brother works at Radio Shack so I'm gonna get all the wiring stuff from them... as you can see, I'm trying to do this as cheaply as possible  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||02-21-2001||07:00 PM||||How do you secure it?  What keeps it from either falling or at least moving out of place?<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||02-21-2001||11:47 PM||||Friction  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  well, at least for me... I was a little imprecise with the plug and it's gonna be a very tight squeeze... Easiest thing to do would be to get some self-adhesive velcro strips and stick 'em on the box.||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||02-22-2001||08:41 AM||||Yep, friction does a great job of holding it in. My box doesn't move around at all. Plus, it's easy to remove when necessary because there is no mounting hardware. But if you are concerned about theft, you could secure it with a small lag bolt and washer to the front bulkhead. Just put it in through the speaker hole.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||02-22-2001||09:32 AM||||Screw radio shack, go ax-man!  8 feet of 6 gauge wire for $1.70!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  woo-hoo!  More than enough for my amp.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Standard||02-23-2001||02:05 AM||||Screw axman!  well, not really axman.. the cheapest connectors i could find for 6 gauge were 10 bucks for a pair, so I just went and ordered an Scosche EFX 10 gauge Amp wiring kit from Crutchfield, 25 bucks shipped.  10 gauge should be able to support up to 800 watts with the short wire length needed. <P>Started laying the fiberglass yesterday, have most of the box covered now.  It's easy once you're used to it.  I'm gonna try to cover the whole box in at least 2 layers.  That's it for now,  I'll keep the updates coming. ||||reg||11
Z||000009||Standard||02-23-2001||11:37 PM||||Got all the box covered, just test fit it.  Did it alot better than I thought, after a bit of pushing it pops right into place.. well, alot of pushing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.  Toughest part is gonna be cutting the front even for the mdf, not straight at all...  gonna go layer some more 'glass on now, though.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Jimmy||02-24-2001||06:23 AM||||   Standard I'm really interested in your sub box.  I'll have to check it out sometime if its all right with you. <BR>   Question:  Just curious, where are you mounting your amp?<P>Jim <BR>87 GT T-TOP<BR>87 GT||||reg||1
Z||000011||Standard||02-24-2001||11:56 AM||||I'm just gonna stick it behind the passenger seat.  Probably just gonna use velcro so I can get at it easily.  You can check it out anytime I'm not working, just give me an e-mail.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Standard||02-27-2001||02:28 PM||||Haven't really done much to the sub box other than layer some more fiberglass on there and try to get the foam out... which is not going to be fun 'cause I used a little too much... ah well..<P>I did get my amp installed, the Pioneer GM-X542.  Wasn't nearly as hard as I thought it would be.  I took pics of everything before I put my interior back together.  Used some self-tapping screws to screw it into the rug behind the seat, doesn't move at all.  Grounded it to the existing ground that's below the ECM next to the passenger seat.  Drilled a hole through the firewall between the 2 big pass-through connectors for the wiring harness, above the ECM.  Definatly seems like the best place to run the power through, I didn't even mess with trying to run it along the shift cable.  I barely used 4 feet of power cable, and about a foot and a half for the ground.  Still have enough cable left over to do one or two more cars  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>It sounds great!  Even just powering the 4x10's, they hit much harder and clearer.  Even though I'm running 50 watts RMS to them and I think they're only rated for 35 watts RMS.. hehe.  If they blow up I just have a reason to get dual 4" speakers  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I hope to have the sub box done by the end of the week, gonna try to get most of the 'glassing finished up tonight after I get off work. ||||reg||1
